# Local-first reproducible notebook workspace

**Status:** candidate-authored design proposal; implementation/integration unverified. Package pins are starting points, not a resolved lock. See `sources.json`, `witnesses.json`, `leads.json`, and `out/critique/review.md`.

## User and outcome

Serve three Python notebook users and a SQL colleague doing exploratory analysis over CSV, JSON/NDJSON and Parquet sources, some larger than RAM. Support import and identification, schema-aware bounded preview, interactive analysis, clean replay, provenance comparison, portable save/reopen and export. Preserve standard notebook and data interchange files. A preview never proves a property of the complete source.

The target host is 8 CPU cores / 16 GB RAM; 5 GB is a target input size for named streaming or spill-capable operations, not a benchmark or promise for arbitrary queries. Fail visibly when an operation exceeds a resource or format boundary.

## Platform choice and compatibility

Use JupyterLab as the UI, Jupyter Server locally, `.ipynb`/nbformat as notebook representation, nbclient for clean execution, Python as the supported kernel, and DuckDB as an optional embedded SQL engine. Defer arbitrary kernels and third-party extensions until reviewed. Candidate direct pins from captured package metadata: Python 3.12 (patch/build TBD), `jupyterlab==4.6.4`, `jupyter-server==2.21.1`, `nbclient==0.11.0`, `nbformat==5.11.1`, `duckdb==1.5.0`; resolve `ipykernel` and every transitive dependency in a platform-specific lock. JupyterLab 4.6.4 declares `jupyter-server>=2.19,<3`, and nbclient 0.11.0 declares `nbformat>=5.2`, so these pairs fit metadata constraints. This does not establish a successful install or runtime integration. Captured Jupyter Server v2.22 docs are ahead of observed PyPI release 2.21.1; validate security configuration on the pinned server.

`.ipynb` gives a standard document with cells and outputs, not a complete execution history, environment, source identity, or proof that outputs are current [S1]. Keep the format and add a project/run manifest. Python-only materialization is easy for small data but risks whole-input memory growth. SQL-only excludes Python exploration. Use Python and optional SQL, with explicit resource policy for both. Server authentication protects access to the service; it is not a kernel sandbox [S3].

## Project and evidence model

A project is an ordinary portable folder containing notebooks, scripts, configuration, environment lock, source references, and append-only run records. Store data sources externally and read-only by default; bundles must state which external inputs are omitted. On open, verify expected input identities and flag missing or changed sources. Record locator, format/options, byte length, full-source SHA-256 when readable, import time, schema revision, and access mode. A digest identifies bytes, not publisher truth. Never rewrite source files.

For each run record run ID, immutable notebook revision digest, input digests, environment-lock digest, engine/runtime versions, parameters, timing, observed cell order/status/source hashes, output digests, and diagnostic references. Separate document order, supervisor-observed execution order, and live kernel state. Missing events or direct execution outside the supervisor make provenance incomplete. Saved notebook outputs are snapshots, not proof of provenance.

Use source digest plus logical record ordinal (and source file identity for multi-file input) for diagnostics; do not claim it is a stable semantic row key across arbitrary exports. Derived artifacts carry schema, producing run and source references. Export creates a new artifact and does not overwrite original data.

## Data contracts and preview

DuckDB documents CSV schema/type inference and strict/error options [S4], JSON readers with inference or declared columns [S7], and Parquet readers/writers and pushdown [S8]. Treat inference as a suggestion. Persist parser, encoding, null markers, schema, cast policy, timestamp/timezone interpretation and malformed-record policy. Show row- and byte-bounded previews with explicit truncation, inferred schema/ambiguity, and labels that counts describe the preview only. Offer a separate complete scan/profile operation with progress and cancellation.

Preserve identifiers such as `001` as text unless the user explicitly casts. Distinguish empty string, configured null marker and null; preserve Unicode; make timestamp/timezone rules explicit. Schema drift creates a new version and requires a visible decision. Malformed row or cast errors fail with a source locator where possible; never silently drop rows. An opt-in reject artifact may preserve raw record and reason; the input stays untouched. DuckDB CSV docs describe strict mode as true and ignore_errors as false by default; do not disable them invisibly [S4]. Parquet multi-file `union_by_name` is an explicit option documented as false by default [S8]. JSON reader support does not promise arbitrary nested documents map to a stable flat schema [S7].

## Interactive work, replay, freshness

Supervise execution in an isolated worker. Record observed cell start/end, cell ID, source digest, order, kernel epoch, status and run/input/environment references. Label displayed output current, stale, failed, interrupted or unverified. Editing a cell stales its result and conservatively makes subsequent cells unverified because notebooks can depend on hidden state. Source, schema or environment changes stale dependent results; reordering invalidates order assumptions. Restart begins a new kernel epoch. Historical outputs remain available but unverified until a new replay.

Clean replay uses an immutable notebook revision, clears outputs in the replay copy, starts a fresh kernel, executes cells sequentially in document order through nbclient, then writes a separate output revision and run ledger. nbclient documents programmatic execution and stop-on-error behavior; continuation can be configured [S2]. Default fail-fast; continuing requires explicit opt-in and per-cell failure markers. Never mark partial/failed output current. Cancel by interrupt, then terminate worker after a bounded interval and record outcome. A nbclient PR fixed kernel cleanup when client startup failed and added a regression test present in v0.7.0 source [S9–S11]. This is a lifecycle precedent, not evidence this proposed supervisor works.

Cache only with complete keys including code, upstream outputs, input digests, parameters, environment and engine settings. Hidden notebook state makes cell-level dependency inference unsafe. First version should cache whole clean runs or conservatively invalidate all later cells; disable caching for interactive or undeclared side-effect runs. A cache reuse links to its immutable original run and exact key.

Replay means re-running declared code/inputs/environment in a fresh worker and comparing results. It cannot promise bit identity for randomness, clocks, nondeterministic concurrency, undeclared I/O/network, external services or platform-sensitive native libraries. Default worker network is disabled; any exception must be declared.

## Security, limits, durability

Notebook code is untrusted. Use a disposable container/VM under an unprivileged identity on each supported host OS. Mount input data read-only; provide separate bounded scratch/output; expose no home directories, credentials, unrelated projects or container socket. Disable network by default; enforce CPU, memory, process count, disk, file size and wall time; supervise/kill process trees; clean temporary state. Import blacklists are not a security boundary. Jupyter Server warns that authorized access permits arbitrary code execution [S3]. Sandbox implementation, host configuration, escape resistance and cleanup are not implemented or tested. Do not run user notebooks until a supported isolation boundary has been validated.

Cap previews by rows and bytes, explain truncation, and make full export deliberate. Put DuckDB spill data in a private bounded directory. DuckDB documents out-of-core processing but warns some combinations of blocking operators and some list/string aggregation/sorting workloads can still exhaust memory; turning insertion-order preservation off may save memory and change ordering [S5]. Thus the 5 GB target requires operation-specific benchmarks. Record relevant settings; results needing order require explicit `ORDER BY` with deterministic tie-breaker. Source scan order does not ensure transformed query order.

Initial persistence is single-writer per project plus read snapshots. Compare revision on concurrent save and require conflict resolution. Save via same-volume temporary file, flush, validate and atomic replace where supported; journal/recover interrupted writes. State weaker guarantees on network/removable filesystems. No multi-host locking guarantee. Keep large results in explicit artifacts, not unbounded notebook output. Store artifact digest/schema/run links and provide CSV/JSON/Parquet export with visible schema/null/order behavior.

## Acceptance plan (proposed, not run)

Resolve full lock; test install/kernel startup/restart/cancel/failure cleanup; test that notebooks cannot read host secrets/unrelated files or reach network; exercise crash recovery and revision conflicts. Cover malformed CSV/JSON/Parquet, schema drift, Unicode, nulls, leading-zero IDs, timestamps, multi-file schema mismatch, stale propagation and clean replay. On the target host profile a representative 5 GB set for scans, filters/projections, joins and blocking aggregates; record peak RSS, spill, time, ordering and failure. Establish operation-specific support limits from results. These are gates, not results.

**Facts:** format structure, documented engine interfaces/limits, package requirements and the nbclient fix are source-backed [S1–S16]. **Inferences:** provenance ledger, stale propagation, isolation and write recovery are proposed mechanisms, not properties established by cited software. **Choices:** target users, direct pins, Python-plus-optional-SQL, one heavy writer, fail-fast replay and offline workers are design choices. **Unknown:** the admitted prior proposal/catalogs could not be resolved in this role; consequently this document cannot claim line-by-line preservation or correction of them. Integrated lock, supported OS, sandbox behavior, data contracts, 5 GB workload envelope and durability are open; see report and leads.